    "discipline": "Chemical",
    "subject": "Chemistry and Biology",
    "chapter": "Biochemistry, Microbiology, and Molecular Biology",
    "topic": "Genetics",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<p>Genes are segments of DNA that encode the instructions for building and maintaining an organism. They are the basic units of heredity. Genes are located on chromosomes, which are thread-like structures composed of DNA and proteins. In humans, there are 46 chromosomes in each cell, organized into 23 pairs. Genotype refers to an individual's genetic makeup, while phenotype refers to the observable characteristics resulting from the interaction of genotype with the environment.</p>",
    "important": "",
    "resources": "",
