    "discipline": "Chemical",
    "subject": "Mathematics",
    "chapter": "Accuracy, Precision, and Significant Figures",
    "topic": "Accuracy and Precision",
    "subtopic": "Accuracy vs Precision",
    "content_html": "<div class=\"notes-image-container my-4 text-center flex flex-col items-center\"><center><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/High_precision_Low_accuracy.svg\" alt=\"High precision Low accuracy\" width=\"200\"><br/>\r\n<p>High Precision but Low Accuracy</p>\r\n<small><small><a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:High_precision_Low_accuracy.svg\">DarkEvil</a>, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons</small></small></center><br/></div><p>If a measurement is consistently giving same values when the measuement is repeated, it is said be precise.</p>\r\n<p>Precise does not mean accurate. As shown in the figure, the measurements are precise but not accurate.</p>",
    "important": "",
