    "discipline": "Chemical",
    "subject": "Thermodynamics",
    "chapter": "Chemical Equilibrium",
    "topic": "Le Chateliers Principle",
    "subtopic": "Overview",
    "content_html": "<h5><span><b>Le Chatelier's Principle:</b></span></h5>\r\n\r\n<p>When a system at equilibrium is subjected to an external change, the system adjusts itself to partially counteract the effect of the applied change, restoring a new state of equilibrium.</p>\r\n\r\nChanges and Equilibrium Shifts:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Concentration Changes:</li>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>If the concentration of a reactant or product is increased, the equilibrium shifts to the opposite side to consume or produce that component until a new equilibrium is established.</li>\r\n<li>If the concentration is decreased, the equilibrium shifts toward the side with the decreased concentration.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<li>Temperature Changes:</li>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>For exothermic reactions (release heat), increasing temperature shifts the equilibrium toward the reactants to absorb heat.</li>\r\n<li>For endothermic reactions (absorb heat), increasing temperature shifts the equilibrium toward the products to absorb more heat.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<li>Pressure Changes (for Gaseous Reactions):</li>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Increasing pressure shifts the equilibrium toward the side with fewer moles of gas.</li>\r\n<li>Decreasing pressure shifts the equilibrium toward the side with more moles of gas.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n</ul>",
    "important": "",
    "resources": "",
    "images": [],
    "local_images": [],
    "video": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/1WVtB9pcKdFYuedz_OcHMC5wJOsjgUjzo/preview",
